summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Beverly Davis is a 24-year-old female who presents for a yearly exam. The patient has a history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension. The patient reports that her blood sugars have been elevated. She has been compliant with her medications as prescribed. She notes that her diet has been poor lately due to her new job as a baker, which has resulted in her snacking and eating more desserts.

The patient reports that her blood pressure has been good. She has been sleeping well, getting a full 8 hours of sleep each night. She denies any concerns about hurting herself.

The patient states that her blood sugars have been up and down. She denies any other concerns.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or unintentional weight loss.
• Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle weakness.
• Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.
• Endocrine: Endorses elevated blood sugars.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: Well-appearing, in no acute distress.
• Skin: No rashes or lesions.
• HEENT: Normocephalic, atraumatic. No scleral icterus.
• Neck: Supple, no thyromegaly, no carotid bruits.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, grade 2 systolic ejection fraction murmur.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally.
• Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no hepatosplenomegaly.
• Extremities: No edema, pulses are 2+ bilaterally.
• Neurological: Alert and oriented, no focal deficits.

RESULTS

Hemoglobin A1c: Elevated.

VITALS REVIEWED

Blood pressure: Within normal limits.
Heart rate: Within normal limits.
Respiratory rate: Within normal limits.
Temperature: Within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT

1. Type 2 diabetes, poorly controlled.
• Medical Reasoning: Her hemoglobin A1c is elevated, indicating that her blood sugars have been poorly controlled. She has been compliant with metformin, but she has been eating a poor diet.
• Patient Education and Counseling: I advised her to reduce her snacking and avoid additional sweets. I also advised her to check her blood sugars in the morning and call them in in 2 weeks. We will re-evaluate in 2 months. If her blood sugars remain elevated, we will consider adding another medication.
• Medical Treatment: No changes to her current medication at this time.

2. Hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: Her blood pressures have been within normal limits. Her hypertension seems to be stable.
• Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 10 mg once daily.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan. }}<|eot_id|>
