Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Rebecca Davis is a 27-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right knee pain.

The onset of her right knee pain began approximately one week ago. The patient reports that she has recently started swimming more regularly. She notes that the pain is only associated with swimming, specifically when she swims the breaststroke. She denies any trauma or injury. She describes her pain as moderate to severe, and it begins after 2 laps of swimming. She states that she is able to swim 10 laps per session one month ago, but now she can only complete 2 laps before the pain begins. The pain is continuous once it begins and has caused her to avoid swimming.

The patient reports that she has a history of carpal tunnel release surgery two years ago. She has a history of chronic back pain and depression, for which she is currently on medication. She denies any recent exacerbations of her back pain. She reports that her depression has been stable with her current medication regimen. She denies any suicidal or homicidal ideations.

The patient reports that she has been sleeping well, eating well, and has been compliant with her medication regimen. She denies any recent changes in her diet or exercise routine.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain. Denies left knee pain. Denies back pain at this time.
• Neurological: Denies any pain radiating down her legs.
• Psychiatric: Reports depression, currently stable. Denies suicidal or homicidal ideations.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• MSK: Examination of the right knee: No effusion. Pain with full range of motion. Pain with patellar inhibition.

RESULTS

X-rays of the right knee were reviewed and are normal.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Rebecca Davis is a 27-year-old female who presents today for evaluation of right knee pain.

Chondromalacia of the patella.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient presents with right knee pain after swimming. Her symptoms and examination findings are consistent with chondromalacia of the patella.
• Medical Treatment: I advised the patient to avoid any activities that exacerbate her symptoms. I recommend anti-inflammatory medications. I also recommend the use of a knee brace. I advised her to perform straight leg raising exercises to strengthen her quadriceps.

Chronic back pain.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her back pain has been well controlled.
• Medical Treatment: Continue with her current treatment regimen.

Depression.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient reports that her depression is stable.
• Medical Treatment: Continue with her current treatment regimen.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks if her symptoms do not improve. }}<|eot_id|>
