PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Musculoskeletal: Swelling of the elbows bilaterally. Pain to palpation to both elbow joints.
• Skin: Bull's-eye rash on the abdomen on the left-hand side. It is warm to palpation. Non-tender to palpation. No evidence of cellulitis.

RESULTS

Lyme titer is elevated.

Hematology and immunology panel is within normal limits.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 31-year-old female with a past medical history of type 2 diabetes and hypertension who presents with abnormal lab findings.

1. Newly diagnosed Lyme disease.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient had a positive Lyme titer. She also reports symptoms consistent with Lyme disease such as a bull's-eye rash and joint pain.
• Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe the patient doxycycline 100 mg twice a day for 3 weeks. I will also touch base with an infectious disease doctor to make sure there is nothing else that we need to do. I want to see her again in 1 week for a follow-up for this.

2. Type 2 diabetes.
• Medical Reasoning: The patient is currently on metformin. She is monitoring her blood sugars at home. Her blood sugars have been running a little high and low. I will order a hemoglobin A1c to make sure we do not need to make any changes to the metformin.

3. Hypertension.
