summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Hypertension and palpitations.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents for a new patient evaluation of hypertension.

The patient was recently seen in the emergency room with elevated blood pressure and palpitations. He believes that his blood pressure was elevated because he had consumed several cups of coffee and did not eat breakfast. He denies feeling palpitations since that day.

The patient has a history of hypertension. He reports that he has had issues with his blood pressure since he was young. He has a family history of hypertension. His blood pressure has been elevated in the past, but he believes that it is now better controlled. He has been on amlodipine 10 mg daily and benicar hct 20-12.5 mg daily. He is also on a water pill. He has been on this regimen for about a month. His girlfriend reminds him to take his medication.

The patient checks his blood pressure daily. His blood pressure is usually 146-155/90-95 mmHg. He tries to limit his salt intake and walks on the treadmill when he can.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Cardiovascular: Reports palpitations. Denies chest pain.
• Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath.
• Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea or vomiting.
• Musculoskeletal: Denies muscle or joint pain.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Pulmonary: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.

RESULTS

Potassium 1 week ago was 4.0.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Jordan Roberts is a 49-year-old male who presents for a new patient evaluation of hypertension.

Resistant hypertension.
• Medical Reasoning: He is on three medications and maximum dose. His potassium is 4.0.
• Medical Treatment: I have added spironolactone 100 mg daily. We will need to monitor his potassium and sodium. He will have a metabolic panel and blood pressure check in 2 weeks. Next medication would be carvedilol, which I try to avoid giving, it's potential for erectile dysfunction. I have talked to the patient about that, and the potential side effects of spironolactone, including the gynecomastia and electrolyte disturbances. If he is not controlled on spironolactone, the next step would be sending him to resistant hypertension clinic.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will see him in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
