summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Christopher Watson is an 86-year-old male who presents in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. The patient has been followed by me since 05/2020. The patient first presented with 8-10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a Spino catheter protocol in 06/2020, and he underwent shunt surgery on 08/01/2020. A Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in a ventricular-peritoneal configuration was programmed at a level of 2.0 and placed.

The patient reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. His walking has improved. He is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance, which he was not able to do prior to surgery. He still has times where he needs assistance. His bladder control improved some after surgery but has leveled out, and he is now wearing a diaper. He denies any issues with sores or decreased/painful urination. His cognition has also improved, and he feels he is better able to carry on conversations.

The patient was asked if he has had any headaches or pain where the shunt is, and he denies both. He also denies any abdominal pain.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Hydrocephalus.

SOCIAL HISTORY

Patient reports he is a retired mechanic. He enjoys watching TV and playing cards.

FAMILY HISTORY

Patient reports his sister had Alzheimer's disease.

MEDICATIONS

Patient reports taking Lisinopril 10 mg daily.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, unintentional weight loss.
Genitourinary: Reports urinary incontinence and wearing a diaper. Denies dysuria or painful output.
Neurological: Reports walking impairment and cognitive impairment. Denies headaches, recent falls, or hallucinations.
Psychiatric: Denies depression, excessive worrying, or mood swings.

VITALS

BP: 124/80.
Heart rate: 64.
Respirations: 18.
Weight: 174 lbs.
Pain: 0/10.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurological: The patient is pleasant and cooperative. He is able to converse easily through his daughter, who adds some details. The shunt site was clean, dry, and intact with a confirmed setting of 2.0. He was tested for recent and remote memory, attention span, and concentration. He scored a 26/30 on the MMSE when tested with spelling and 25/30 when tested with calculations. He was able to get 2 of 3 memory words with cuing and the third with multiple choice. This was a slight improvement over his initial score of 23/30 with calculations and 24/30 with spelling. At the time, he was unable to remember any memory words with cuing without and only 1 with multiple choice. Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool was performed without an assistive device. He received a gait score of 6-8/12 and a balance score of 12/16 for a total score of 18/20-28. This is slightly improved from his initial score of 15-17/28. Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face is symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor: Normal bulk and strength. Coordination: Slow from nose to finger. Of note, the CT scan from 10/15/2020 shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extraaxial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus.

PLAN

Given the tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region and the improvement in symptoms, we will continue to monitor for another 2-3 months before we consider shunt revision. We will schedule the patient to return in 3 months for follow-up. The patient was educated about his condition and the rationale for this approach. The patient was also advised that he may not be able to drive at this time and that he could enroll in a driver safety program without my recommendation. All questions were answered.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3 months. }}<|eot_id|>
