Amanda Taylor is a 72-year-old female who presents for follow-up of hypertension. Her history includes significant alcohol use disorder and mild hypercholesterolemia. At her last visit on 04-16-2021, her blood pressure was 130/90 mmHg. She presented today with a blood pressure of 150/100 mmHg.

The patient reports that she has been drinking alcohol 4 to 5 days per week. She states that she is taking half the dose of lisinopril 10 mg daily due to lethargy and headaches. She reports that she has been taking the full dose of hydrochlorothiazide 12.5 mg daily. She denies chest tightness, shortness of breath, or any other symptoms. She is taking her blood pressure at home.

PHYSICAL EXAMINATION

General: Skin is moist and warm, no edema in the lower extremities.
CV: No murmur, no gallop, regular rate and rhythm.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally, unlabored breathing.
Neurological: No focal deficits, no tremor, no diabetes.

RESULTS

CMP and CBC were essentially unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT

Alcohol use disorder.
Hypertension.
Hypercholesterolemia.

PLAN

We discussed the potential for Naltrexone to help with her alcohol cravings. She would like to start the injectable form and start on 05-17-2021. She is aware of the potential side effects and risks. We discussed the potential benefits of alcohol cessation. Her hypertension is likely to improve if she stops drinking. She will follow up with me in week of 06-14-2021, which is likely around the time of her second Naltrexone injection.

Order for influenza and Shingrix vaccination, which she deferred.

INSTRUCTIONS

She will follow up with me in week of 06-14-2021. }}<|eot_id|>
