summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for perioral dermatitis.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a 35-year-old female who presents to the clinic for follow-up of her perioral dermatitis. The patient reports that she has been using doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for the past 2 weeks. The patient has noticed improvement but continues to have some residual rash. She has been using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily and metrocream after washing her face. The patient denies breastfeeding and is not currently breastfeeding.

The patient states that she stopped breastfeeding and then asked for doxycycline at that time. She was previously concerned about using elidel, which she read about. I explained to the patient that the information she read was not relevant to the topical form of elidel, but rather the oral form used in immunosuppressed patients, such as those post-transplant. I reassured her that doxycycline is low risk.

The patient has been using the sulfacetamide wash twice daily. She denies dryness or thinning of the skin from this use.

The patient has been using metrocream after washing her face. I explained to the patient that she could try tacrolimus ointment as an alternative. The patient stated that she checked with her insurance and found that tacrolimus ointment was not covered. I advised the patient that the prices of tacrolimus ointment can fluctuate, and she should try to use a goodrx coupon to obtain it. I also explained to the patient that tacrolimus ointment is not a steroid, but it is a calcineurin inhibitor. I reassured the patient that it will not cause thinning of the skin, but it may cause a tingling or needle-like sensation for a few seconds after application, which will resolve. The patient stated that she would try to obtain tacrolimus ointment.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Perioral dermatitis.

The patient has been using doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for the past 2 weeks and has noticed improvement. She has been using sulfacetamide wash twice daily and metrocream after washing her face. The patient has been advised to apply tacrolimus ointment to the affected area twice daily until resolved, then once daily for 1 week before discontinuing. She was also advised to continue using sulfacetamide wash once daily and metrocream as needed.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 4 weeks.

Lifestyle recommendations provided. The patient was advised to use a basic moisturizer such as vanicream or petroleum jelly to address dryness. The patient was advised to avoid anti-aging or plant-based products. }}<|eot_id|>
