summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right upper quadrant abdominal pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Bobby Williams is a pleasant 68-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right upper quadrant abdominal pain. He was referred by Dr. Miller who performed an ultrasound on him and found gallstones. He has been advised to avoid high-fat foods, but he continues to experience significant pain. He notes the pain is located on the right side of his abdomen, just under his ribs, and it is described as severe and squeezing in nature. The pain comes and goes, and it is associated with nausea. He rates his pain as an 8 out of 10 when it is severe. The pain is intermittent, but it is usually worse after meals. He is a type 2 diabetic and has been handling this with diet and exercise. He has lost 25 pounds over the last 3 months since he switched to a keto diet. He notes that his symptoms have improved with this diet change. He has a family history of gallbladder disease in both his father and mother, who both had their gallbladder removed.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he is an avid photographer and nature enthusiast. He enjoys going to the woods to take pictures. He is active and enjoys hiking.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Reports low-grade fevers. Denies chills.
Gastrointestinal: Reports nausea, right upper quadrant pain. Denies vomiting.
Respiratory: Denies cough or shortness of breath.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.

VITALS

BP: 128/88 mmHg
RR: 16
Temp: Afebrile
Pulse: 68
SpO2: 99% on room air

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
RESPIRATORY: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
GI: Positive for guarding to the right upper quadrant. No rebound tenderness. No peritoneal signs.

RESULTS

Abdominal ultrasound reveals multiple gallstones present in the gallbladder. Common bile duct size is within normal limits at 4.1 mm.

ASSESSMENT

Mild cholecystitis with gallstones. No evidence of obstruction.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with him in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we proceed with a laparoscopic cholecystectomy. I have explained the procedure, risks, benefits, and recovery expectations. He is in agreement with this plan. We will schedule the procedure and he will spend the night in the hospital for observation postoperatively. He will follow up with me in 1 week and will continue to follow up with his primary care physician for his type 2 diabetes.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week. He will continue to avoid high-fat foods and eat a high-fiber diet postoperatively. He will monitor his blood sugars closely and follow up with his primary care physician for his type 2 diabetes.

All questions were answered. }}<|eot_id|>
