Right knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Gregory Smith is a pleasant 58-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of right knee pain. He reports that he injured his right knee approximately 2.5 weeks ago while stepping off a curb. He denies that he fell to the ground. He states that he has some ankle soreness but not knee pain. He reports that the pain is present with walking, but it is only present when he is bearing weight on his right leg. He denies that he experiences any numbness or tingling. He reports that he is able to bend his knee, but he notes that he feels a clicking sensation when he does so. He reports that he is able to ride a stationary bike but has stopped due to his knee pain. He reports that he enjoys riding his stationary bike to rock and reggae music. The patient reports that he is not a big pill taker and has not been taking any medication for his pain. He reports that he has been using ice to help with the pain. The patient reports that he is able to sleep through the night but notes that his sleep is interrupted if he bears weight on his right leg. He denies that he experiences any numbness or tingling in his right leg. He reports that he has a history of a broken second toe on his right foot and notes that he experiences numbness in his right foot that depends on the type of shoe he is wearing. The patient reports that he has broken his second toe on his right foot 3 times over the past 10 years. He denies that the current knee pain is related to his ankle pain.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness or tingling.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the right knee: Slight effusion. No pain to palpation of the right lateral knee. Positive varus test. Full range of motion. Negative Lachman sign.
CV: Palpable dorsalis pedis and posterior tibialis pulse.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee was obtained and reviewed today. There are no acute fractures or bony abnormalities noted.

ASSESSMENT

Lateral collateral ligament strain of the right knee.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to the patient that his symptoms are likely due to a lateral collateral ligament strain. I have recommended that we refer the patient to physical therapy. I have also recommended that we start the patient on meloxicam 15 mg once daily. I have also recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee if he is still having pain after completing physical therapy. All questions were answered.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 2 weeks. }}<|eot_id|>
