Gregory Johnson is a pleasant 32-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of right ankle pain. The onset of his pain began yesterday after he fell on the ice. The patient states that he was going to take out the trash and slipped on some ice. He denies any numbness in the right foot. The patient reports that he has been able to walk on it a little bit today but is still limping. He states that he has been keeping it elevated and iced, and he has been taking ibuprofen, which has provided him with some relief. The patient is currently playing in the community soccer league and is also a coach for his kids.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient denies any previous injuries to the right ankle.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that he is currently playing in the community soccer league. He is a coach for his kids.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that he has been taking ibuprofen.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right ankle pain and swelling.
Neurological: Denies numbness in the right foot.

VITALS

Vitals are within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Neurovascular exam of the right foot shows that capillary refill is less than 3 seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. Sensation is intact to light touch.

Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right ankle shows ecchymosis and swelling over the lateral malleolus associated with the right ankle. There is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. No laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. No bony tenderness on palpation of the foot.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right ankle was obtained and was unremarkable.

ASSESSMENT

Right ankle sprain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are consistent with a right ankle sprain. I have recommended that he keep his leg elevated and continue to ice the ankle. I have also recommended that he be placed in an aircast to stabilize the ankle. He will also be given crutches for the next 1-2 days and then he may start walking on it as tolerated. We will follow up with him in 2 weeks to see how he is doing. }}<|eot_id|>
