Elevated cholesterol.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Julie Cruz is a 17-year-old non-binary, Hispanic, and currently impersonated minor with no significant past medical history. The patient presents for evaluation of newly diagnosed hypercholesterolemia. The patient was seen at an outside facility for an annual checkup where a finger prick cholesterol test was obtained and was elevated. The patient was advised to follow up with me.

The patient reports that she enjoys outdoor activities such as riding her bicycle. She notes that she prefers to cook her meals using fresh ingredients and admits to enjoying chocolate chip cookies. The patient states that she has been active during the week. She denies any history of hypercholesterolemia in any family members.

The patient is currently living on her own and there is no one in the household who smokes. She reports that she does not recall any history of heart disease or early mortality in her family members.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient denies any history of hypercholesterolemia in family members.

SURGICAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she had a cholesterol level obtained at her last annual checkup and it was borderline elevated.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Constitutional: Denies fevers, chills, or weight loss.
Cardiovascular: Denies chest pain or palpitations.
Respiratory: Denies shortness of breath or cough.
Gastrointestinal: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or constipation.
Genitourinary: Denies dysuria or hematuria.
Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain or muscle weakness.
Neurological: Denies headaches, dizziness, or syncope.
Psychiatric: Denies anxiety or depression.

VITALS

Blood pressure: Normal.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV: No murmur, rub, or gallop.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Eyes: No exophthalmos or conjunctival pallor.
Neck: No thyromegaly.
Abdominal: No hepatomegaly or splenomegaly.

RESULTS

Cholesterol level: Elevated at 210 mg/dL.

ASSESSMENT

1. Elevated cholesterol.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and test results, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient and her guardian regarding her elevated cholesterol level. I have recommended that we obtain a full cholesterol panel, a complete metabolic panel, thyroid studies, and liver panels. I have also recommended genetic testing to determine if this is a familial or secondary cause. We will follow up with a well-balanced diet, including a variety of foods that are lower in saturated fats and sugars. I have also recommended that the patient meet with a nutritionist for dietary counseling. We will follow up in 3-6 months for a repeat cholesterol level after the patient has met with the nutritionist and dietary changes have been instituted.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up in 3-6 months for a repeat cholesterol level after dietary changes have been instituted. }}<|eot_id|>
