summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Low back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Kyle Davis is a pleasant 29-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. The onset of his pain began 2 weeks ago while he was lifting a box of books. He denies proper lifting technique. The patient describes the pain as aching and sharp in nature localized to his low back. The pain initially did not radiate, but now it does radiate down his left leg. The patient denies any numbness, tingling, or loss of sensation. He denies any weakness. He also denies any loss of bladder or bowel control. The patient states that his pain has been worsening. He tried taking ibuprofen and Tylenol with occasional relief. The patient denies any prior discussion of surgery. His activity level is low; he is an Olympic weightlifter but is currently inconsistent in his exercise routine. He has a sedentary job. The patient denies tobacco use. He has a history of playing soccer and is a Tatsan Hotspur fan.

FAMILY HISTORY

The patient reports that his father has back pain.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, loss of sensation, or weakness.
Genitourinary: Denies loss of bladder or bowel control.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 130/70.
Respiratory rate: 18.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
Musculoskeletal: Examination of the back reveals no overlying erythema or bruising. Palpation of the midline of the back elicits tenderness at the L4-L5 disk space. Right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Pain with lumbar extension. Straight leg test is positive.
Neurological: Decreased sensation to light touch in the right lower extremity. Reflexes are 2+ and symmetrical at the patella and Achilles.

RESULTS

MRI of the lumbar spine reveals a disk desiccation with paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Kyle Davis is a 29-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of low back pain. His symptoms are in line with lumbar back pain at L4-L5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy. I have recommended spinal injection with a strong NSAID and corticosteroid, which we will administer into the lumbar spine under fluoroscopy. All questions were answered. The patient agrees with the plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 1 week. }}<|eot_id|>
