summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Angela Johnson is a 36-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began 1 week ago when she was running around with her 5-year-old niece who is learning how to take off the training wheels. She states she fell and twisted her knee in a funky way. She denies hearing a pop at the time of the injury. She is currently unable to bear weight on her left leg and is limping. She describes the pain as a 4/10. She denies the use of NSAIDs due to an ulcer and is currently taking Tylenol and wrapping her knee with kinesiology tape. She denies that the Tylenol has been helping.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema present, effusion present, moderate ROM, pain with flexion and extension of the knee, negative Lachman's test, negative valgus and varus test.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left knee is normal. No fractures or bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have assessed her with an MCL strain of her left knee. I have recommended that she take tramadol 50 mg twice a day for pain. She can take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. I have recommended that she wear a knee immobilizer for 1 week. I have also recommended that she ice her knee 3 to 4 times a day for 20 to 30 minutes at a time. I have advised her to refrain from running for 3 weeks.

Patient agrees with the plan and will follow up in 1 month. }}<|eot_id|>
